          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This happens because of more "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "saliva",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " that gets released when you recall your favourite food."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.123,
            0.744,
            0.179
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "What do you think is the role of saliva in your mouth? What do you feel when you eat other types of food, such as chapati? Let us find out."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.18,
            0.744,
            0.274
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Take a small piece of chapati or a bite-sized portion of boiled rice and chew it properly for 30–60 seconds. At first, the chapati or rice has its usual taste, but as you continue chewing, do you notice a change in taste? The food begins to taste sweet! Have you ever wondered why this happens?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.275,
            0.744,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapati or rice contains starch, which is a type of carbohydrate. Our saliva contains a digestive juice that helps break down starch into sugar. This explains why starchy food, like chapati, tastes sweet when you chew it for a long time. Saliva helps to break down components of food into simpler ones."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.388,
            0.362,
            0.407
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Science and Society"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.411,
            0.685,
            0.483
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A healthy mouth requires good oral hygiene. We should brush our teeth and clean our tongue twice a day, and rinse our mouth with water after each meal to prevent tooth decay and bad smell in the mouth. Find out the ways our elders were maintaining oral hygiene."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
